# Portable publication resources

Text alone is easy to move. A book with illustrations, diagrams, and chosen fonts
is portable only when those resources travel under explicit rules. Copying an
entire project directory would be simple, but it would also publish unrelated
files, hide missing licenses, and preserve unsafe author-controlled paths.

Literate instead plans a small companion set. Every resource is declared or found
through validated document structure, read through a narrow filesystem adapter,
assigned a safe publication name, and joined to the TeX document before any output
is committed.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Book[Validated publication document] --> Discover[Discover requested resources]
    Manifest[Explicit font manifest] --> Validate[Validate every declared companion]
    Discover --> Read[Read policy-checked local inputs]
    Validate --> Read
    Read --> Name[Assign safe content or role identities]
    Name --> Plan[Complete resource plan]
    Plan --> Transaction[Publish TeX and companions together]
```

The arrows show planning dependencies. They do not mean that discovery copies files
immediately. The transaction receives only a complete in-memory plan. The diagram
omits external diagram rendering, which is an optional producer feeding the same
naming and planning stage.

## Paths are capabilities, not decoration

A resource path grants permission to read a project file, so it is validated before
filesystem access. Paths are relative to a known root, normalized with portable
slash rules, and forbidden from escaping through parent traversal. Absolute paths,
backslashes, drive syntax, control characters, URL schemes, queries, and fragments
are rejected where local publication inputs are expected.

A standalone Markdown image is resolved relative to its source chapter. URI
escaping is decoded exactly once, and both encoded and decoded forms are checked.
This prevents a second decoding layer from changing the apparent path after policy
has accepted it.

The host verifies every existing path component, rejects symbolic links for project
resources, and requires regular files. Image and diagram adapters also enforce their
fixed byte budgets before and after reading. Font and license companions currently
have path, manifest, presence, and file-kind checks, but no dedicated byte or count
budget.

## Images remain explicit and bounded

Publication accepts standalone PNG, JPEG, and PDF illustrations. The filename
extension must agree with a recognizable binary signature. This is a format sanity
check, not a complete malware or document-validity scan; project resources are
trusted publication inputs.

Image titles become plain captions, falling back to plain alternative text. Inline
images, formatted alternatives, remote URLs, raw SVG files, queries, and fragments
are rejected. A PDF illustration contributes its first page, so an author who needs
a single figure is responsible for supplying an appropriate file.

Identical image bytes are stored once even when reached through different source
paths. Every source path remains a protected input, because deduplication must not
make another alias eligible as an output destination.

## Content-derived names remove author syntax

Companion directories and files use hashes rather than source basenames. A content
hash makes equal bytes share one companion and prevents spaces, path separators, or
TeX punctuation from entering the generated reference.

For the bounded image and diagram companions, if the unique byte lengths are `b₁`
through `bₙ`, the retained resource cost is

```text
B = b₁ + b₂ + … + bₙ
```

Publication accepts these companions only while `B` stays within fixed
implementation budgets. Counting unique bytes reflects actual retained output,
while a separate request-count bound limits work caused by many aliases. Exceeding
either bound rejects the plan; no image or diagram is silently omitted. This
formula does not describe font or license companions, which do not yet have an
equivalent aggregate budget.

Content-derived names do not prove authenticity and are not a cache lifetime
promise. They provide deterministic, syntax-safe companion identities for one
planned publication.

```NOTE
A matching hash says that two byte sequences are treated as the same content here.
It does not say who created those bytes, whether they are trustworthy, or whether
they are licensed for publication.
```

`publication asset addition` gives retained bytes a deterministic, syntax-safe
identity.

```ts : <<publication asset addition>>=
const add = (content: Uint8Array, extension: string): string => {
    const companion = `${directory}/${hash(content)}.${extension}`;
    if (!files.has(companion)) {
        total += content.length;
        if (total > 128 * 1024 * 1024) { throw new Error('Publication images exceed 128 MiB'); }
        files.add(companion);
        plan.files.push({ path: posix.join(posix.dirname(output), companion), content });
    }
    return companion;
};
```

## Diagrams are rendered as optional assets

A Mermaid fence is a request for a vector figure, not ordinary code in the printed
book. Books without diagrams need no browser. Books with diagrams require an
explicitly selected, trusted Chromium executable and an isolated renderer process.
No browser is discovered, installed, or downloaded on the author's behalf.

The only author-controlled payload crossing the worker boundary is bounded diagram
source plus a catalogue theme. The controller also supplies its private directory
and the explicitly selected Chromium path. Author frontmatter, directives, raw
HTML, CSS commands, and interaction commands are rejected. The browser runs fixed
local code, denies network activity, and validates the resulting SVG before the
controller accepts it.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Fence[Mermaid source] --> Policy[Validate bounded source policy]
    Policy --> Worker[Private browser worker]
    Worker --> SVG[Validated vector SVG]
    Worker --> PDF[Validated vector PDF]
    SVG --> Companions[Resource plan]
    PDF --> Companions
```

The PDF is the figure used by LaTeX; the SVG preserves a portable vector companion.
The arrows represent bytes returned to the planner, not browser writes into the
project. Identical source strings render once for the selected theme.

The worker owns a private directory, browser profile, and process group. Time,
source, artifact, total-byte, and log bounds turn a stuck or excessive renderer into
a failed publication rather than an unbounded background task. Cleanup is part of
the result: undisclosed leftover profiles are not treated as success.

## Fonts require an explicit manifest

Font discovery is intentionally absent. Scanning a directory or querying installed
families would make the publication depend on accidental machine state and would
not explain whether redistribution is allowed.

A manifest is an explicit allowlist. Each family declares regular, bold, italic,
and bold-italic faces plus at least one license file. Unknown fields are errors so a
misspelled face or license key cannot disappear silently. Font paths use the same
portable root-relative policy and name supported font containers.

All manifest entries are validated and all declared files are required to exist,
including entries not selected by the current book. Only selected families are
copied. This makes the manifest a coherent declared set while keeping the output
small.

A face normally names a real font file. Italic faces may explicitly request a
bounded synthetic slant while still naming the source file. There is no implicit
font fallback, synthetic weight, arbitrary font-feature string, or attempt to infer
a missing face.

## License files travel with selected fonts

Every selected family's license files are copied as opaque bytes into the companion
set. The planner verifies presence; it does not interpret legal terms or decide
whether redistribution is permitted. That judgment remains with the publisher.

The distinction is important. Requiring a license companion prevents accidental
omission, while pretending to automate legal interpretation would offer false
assurance.

```NOTE
Shipping a license file is record-keeping, not legal advice. The software can prove
that the declared notice travelled with the font; the publisher must still decide
whether the intended redistribution is permitted.
```

Font companion names derive from family and face roles rather than author paths.
The renderer receives only those safe names and the validated synthetic-slant value,
if any. A raw project path never enters a font command.

## Validate all destinations before conversion

Document sources, declared templates, image aliases, the font manifest, font files,
license files, and an explicitly chosen browser are protected inputs. Planned
outputs are checked against them for equality and ancestry in both directions.
Existing hard-link identity is checked as well as lexical spelling.

The planner also checks outputs against one another. A destination cannot be both a
file and a parent directory, and case-folded equivalents are rejected even on a
case-sensitive host. These checks happen before Pandoc conversion and before an
output transaction creates directories.

## Portability has a defined boundary

A portable LaTeX publication consists of the TeX document and its planned companion
directories. Move the containing output directory as a unit. Old companions that no
longer belong to a newer plan are not deleted automatically; automatic pruning
could remove files owned by another publication.

The package still depends on a suitable TeX installation. Without a font manifest,
it also depends on the selected families being installed. Diagram production needs
the explicitly configured browser, but compiling an already prepared diagram does
not. No shell escape is required for images or diagrams.

Portability therefore means that declared project resources are copied under safe,
stable names and referenced relatively. It does not mean that every compiler,
package, glyph, or legal permission is embedded.

Once the complete TeX and resource plan exists, PDF production can happen in a
private build. The next chapter follows that plan through external tools while
preserving the previous published output until a replacement is fully accepted.
